      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 AAGL. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Abnormal placentation is the most common indication for peripartum hysterectomy. To date, the approach described in the literature is laparotomy, which is associated with high morbidity and mortality. A 30-year-old gravida 4 para 3 had a postpartum diagnosis of placenta percreta. She was first treated conservatively. On day 3 after delivery, because of persistent vaginal bleeding, she underwent a laparoscopic hysterectomy. No postoperative complications occurred, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day 3. Laparoscopic peripartum hysterectomy could become the approach of choice in selected patients with abnormal placentation to avoid complications associated with laparotomy."
      },
